6149988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a36f86a-9cf0-5a74-9e73-0b6b6057dd12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149989 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8e7f094-f20a-53ac-ad68-c1b836150308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149991 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149991 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149991 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15a8bf83-6bed-5f2d-a921-4c1c41806500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149992 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149992 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149992 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39f0bda6-54c7-5b33-a8c3-6a85937caa55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151943 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151943 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/fb/fb428b64d8d1fc020f343aea11f50ad92158ad4dcbf611b5decb44bd79ac221c.svg) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151943 #edb/ref 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/7a/7abb144e511574809625c59d5ed6b7658983fc646bd0e6d6327f9f12356b8f08.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151944 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151944 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/b2/b22e2ffa6f6d88665e120e8dad82961e72f8f4a99484e8f81de311a66ab25988.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/28/28f79983a195fa0152832aebd334cd8e1aaaa977dd9016347a3152403fd390c7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151944 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/45/4588c7d7ae4fd6cbc749373d83ab2d81e6a228f0cac03d5b959f4177f3f4cdf9.svg)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/fa/fa2ad666a6b80eac26bad12db322fa361578d94327e230d7c520c950add78923.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151945 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151945 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/af/afbd6ce90f2192c311c38ced9c1d9512c48bbf9a5e06236415ef9fe70883f9be.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2ccba053acf8d050e305c3768c970ae635f4583163a867b6ef11f4e565ac1ae.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151945 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/6b/6b4306172b78c1e59bf66c8c08bf864aac2bcab65d4b1cc08ba4ddab0e5f1da3.svg)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/b3/b3681d7f6c71d06ede32616ceec12ccd71da28373ee88eab451211af5a579212.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152155 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152156 #edb