ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152330 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7d3eac14-632f-5a58-a023-44f29da9dd83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152579 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152579 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c555c8c9-de13-50dd-99c1-9c9975c94651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152579 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 3=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152580 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${a}_{2}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40c35237-5ebd-5668-8d9f-8241b6761c15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152580 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{13}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=3\\cdot 0=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153053 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153053 #edb/ref 1061 23536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153053 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89804eda-a3ae-5485-b48e-d4ab4a5760e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153053 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153054 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153054 #edb/ref 1061 23537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153054 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "675dd937-a474-5a45-ab38-929745f4d3a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153054 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153055 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153055 #edb/ref 1061 23538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153055 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bd1dbe7-2b70-5718-9b1c-1634535fb285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153055 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153056 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153056 #edb/ref 1061 23539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153056 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "665e0a4a-ccbf-5a7f-9823-1d0163d85f44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153056 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153057 #edb/ref 1061 23569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4fcbb627-ddcb-5a6f-aa6f-8950fb6d730a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153352 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n![](images/6f/6f8184c584bac2ae9f4f0655d6de2f05bae48142caa729cc24faa30eb5e38774.svg)\n\nthe column space is\n\n![](images/83/83a4941812514b331349e96637ba4fc3d9686e85704506855f931efc3ed3d539.svg)\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153352 #edb/ref 1065 3965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153352 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "141672d1-fa24-513d-ba34-8cbf5a1d0b84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153352 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153353 #edb/ref 1064 "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n![](images/c8/c8e10a79d647ed7867d42eae1eff7d37840f541cfce26e47d84f66da4e9f0c96.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n![](images/84/84cdf6af82bd0d301ca50b1c7c41bccbd0a3cfb42be17bc84ec2f12e46032161.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/22/22d4eb11af0168d4fabc8b3ebbe1befd24620db11b6a31709472ba91bb1ed794.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/f3/f3f4e0d48c58be10684eada3fd320001dc056f2d1070707d9443c9533b7b5e35.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/cf/cf66dc341420f6c399e1ad7e108986505cb349d3aba1aeaacee636e7b6ec4b94.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/84/847a4dd2e37e6e577bd9a38160f03bcb5839fbf617d76ebe0a2b42cdba0ebce1.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n![](images/18/1892b80eb017795a7420b59053b836bea894ff77b7478fc7f884de6b43384cb1.svg)\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153353 #edb/ref 1065 3966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153353 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cb82414a-0cb2-5363-9301-c8d79896bc22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153353 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244311 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244311 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244311 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a32f43-637b-5461-9562-d1f4f4633f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244349 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "045d9074-4003-5b5e-b5db-6e1652c4adc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244423 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244423 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244423 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bad38f8-0d9a-5b24-af08-76251c6d46d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244593 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218