#edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246971 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246971 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246971 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186245769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246971 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ece770f6-1100-5fc9-b657-fa1005c33ce5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247227 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $C$ is given below. Let ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$,  ${c}_{3}$, and ${c}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $C.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(C).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${c}_{4}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a52283aa-8e43-5623-8f72-5c841a8ea5a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247227 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $C$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$ is $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${c}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{4}}={{(4)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot 3=12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247228 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if \n$B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "840e6c59-db8d-561a-a36d-d9c509886863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247228 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-12){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is $\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247233 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $S$ is given below. Let ${s}_{1}$, ${s}_{2}$,  ${s}_{3}$ and ${s}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $S.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${s}_{4}={x}_{1}{s}_{1}+{x}_{2}{s}_{2}+{x}_{3}{s}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fa86926-dc53-59ca-bada-4e3c0c5873bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247233 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & 