gned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345139 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$, and ${p}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{3}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edfd11b7-178d-5b9f-89aa-f777ae4db963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345139 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{3}}={{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-2)\\cdot 1=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345143 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345143 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345143 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345143 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e38527f-7273-52c9-bec3-d94fc3af2837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345143 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345149 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345149 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345149 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345149 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$,  ${h}_{3}$ and ${h}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${h}_{4}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{2}{h}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2ee7dc8-6fbf-5fd7-b2a6-20aab9680dc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345149 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 